Z||000006||Taijiguy||03-29-2001||08:55 AM||||I'm sure you are feeling daunted by the list, and my suggestion is to take a deep breath, and accept the fact that you are where you are, and can only go forward. You only got four choices: fix it, drive it as is, sell it, or park it until you can emotionally and financially handle fixing it. As for putting another 500 bucks into a 500 buck car and having it be only worth 800 bucks: if you bought it as an investment, you probably screwed up. Unless you're going to sink a buttload of money into it to make it perfect, or completely custom, forget getting your money back out of it. As investments go, in general, cars aren't the best choice. (In my experience) For what I've put into mine so far, I could have bought a new car. And it's not even done. I'll never see that money again. I don't care, I like my Fiero more than any new car I've driven lately. As for the problems with your car:<BR>I would agree that the clunking could be the motor mount. Drive the car in like second gear, run it to about 2500 rpm, then jab the accelerator for a second and then get all the way off it. (this should cause the motor to twist) You should hear the noise then. Check them all, especially the dogbone at the top. As for the shifter, try adjusting the cables back at the transmission. That may help your shifting problem. Also, maybe change the fluid in the tranny. Alot of problems have been connected to the wrong fluid in there. It should only be 10-30 oil if I recall. Not 80-90 gear lube, that's too heavy for these trannys. Fieros didn't come with power steering....unless your talking about *arm power* Check the fuses first in addressing the electrical problems of course, there's some funky wiring in Fiero, and some unlikely components share fuses. The ground is a good possibility too, bad grounds can result in some really bizarre electrical problems. Hope these help you some in getting started. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000007||GodSend||03-29-2001||12:21 PM||||Dont worry about it... heres a little info from someone who bought a Fiero last fall.<P>1]  The shifting pattern is really tight.  Wehn I first started out it was really hard to tell 2nd from 4th , untill the car started to chug.<P>2]  The door locks are easy.  If you can lock it with a key but not with the inside "slider" then its probably just a missing/unhooked arm .. take the door skin off.<P>3]  Did you check the bulbs for the dash  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.. they might be burnt out...<P>As for the smoking, you could try giving the engine a good cleaning.. who knows what kinds of grime, dirt, leaves, oil could be causing that... <P>4]  No power steering, takes a good amount of effort when parking.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||03-29-2001||01:30 PM||||Check to see if the valve cover is leaking and causing the oil to smoke on the headers.<P>I think Godsend mean't to take the interior door pad off, not the outer skin.  Not a biggie job.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrgone||03-29-2001||01:47 PM||||thanks for all the help, i had a few buddies look at it, it has two oil leaks, the valve cover gasket is shot, and theres a different oil leak.transmission mount is shot, it need plugs (thats normal on a car) oil pressure and temperature send unit, front engine mount, theres a bolt missing on the transmission, o2 sensor pcv valve fuel filter<BR>andd the clutch fluid is very dark.<BR>im screwed at trying to get my money back, i have alot of time till i start my job, and if i look around and get good prices, im sure i could maybe only spend 1000 or whatever to get it certified. those were the first things to look for though, and who know what you can find if you really go looking.||||reg||9
Z||000010||Voytek||03-29-2001||03:36 PM||||Hey<BR>Taijiguy is right.  The rough shifting can be attributed to a couple of things, besides a bad transmission.  First: the fluid recommended is actually 5-W-30, however, GM sells Sincromesh which is even better.  Check the manual for fluid quantity (sometimese the level indicator on the speedometer housing is not correct, I think it depends on the Fiero year) and make sure you have the right qty. (too much may also make it rough).  I would do that first.  Second: there is a procedure to adjust the cable linkage properly.  You have to remove the center console, loosen the nuts on the cables at the transmission, etc, etc.  It is tough to describe the procedure, BUT, if you can get your hands on a Haynes manual, they describe it fairly good (or buy a manual).  These are two small-cost fixins and may improve your shifting significantly.  Other than that sounds like a lot of TLC and a few minor things (engine mounts will give you a clunking noise and they're not very expensive at all and relatively easy to replace with a couple of jackstands, a jack and a couple of bricks to prop the engine under).  $1000 bucks can be not too bad if you do some of the work yourself AND none of the problems are really major (which it doesn't sound like they are).<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
