Z||000015||Gold-86SE||03-29-2001||06:59 PM||||<BR>mrgone, fwiw, I realize the money is tight, but it may be that the usual things just need to be done to this fiero, like on any used automobile.<P>I am trying to help give you a bright spot here, remember take small steps, otherwise it looks like you are trying to eat an elephant.<P>You listed several areas that your buddies saw that needed work.<P>Let me try to give you a ruff estimate in u.s. dollars, hopefully this will help in determining what you want to do.<P>all these prices are autozone, advance auto type stuff and are approximate value based on memory, providing it is working.<P>valve cover gasket - $7.00<BR>theres a different oil leak  - not sure, depends on problems, see the above info about changing the tranny oil.  but there is the possiblity that it may be leaking from a transaxle seal about 4 buck each side, or the o ring on the speed sensor about a buck fifty at pontiac.<P>transmission mount - $14.00<P>plugs - 4 to 5 bucks, buy the cheap ac delco stuff to get the car running better, while your at it replace the plug wires (about $16) and the distributer cap and rotor (another $12-16)<P>*oil pressure - not sure<P>*temperature sending unit - not sure, but the one on my v6 was about 8 bucks<P>front engine mount - $12 - $16<P>missing transmission bolt - ask in the mall, i am sure someone will have one, cheap, if not free with paid postage<P>*o2 sensor - $15<P>pcv valve - $3<P>fuel filter - $5-$10<P>clutch fluid - $4 (just don't bleed the clutch til you read archies technique, I'll post a link in a bit)<P>Your're not really "screwed" you just need to spend a little more on the car.<P>Those things marked with an "*" are usually relayed to you when you check the computer codes.<P><BR>OK, the work that I know I would do up front bar none is the following and is only about 90 bucks.<BR>valve cover gasket - $7.00<BR>plugs - $5 bucks<BR>plug wires - $16<BR>distributer cap and rotor $16<BR>transmission mount - $14<BR>front engine mount - $16<BR>pcv valve - $3<BR>fuel filter - $10<P>Do that work, then check your codes and see what happens.  You may be in for a pleasant surprise.||||reg||1
Z||000016||DavidHenshaw||03-30-2001||01:36 AM||||You've got some great replys so far but I'd like to emphasize a couple things. <P>1. There are very few autos that look as good as the Fiero. You did good there.<P>2. You start a good job soon so you'll have some money to correct the problems<P>3. The car is probably not as bad as it seems.<P>4. You can always register it in Kentucky. If it stands upright it passes. Just kidding, if you're in Kentucky don't flame me.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000017||JohnnyK||03-30-2001||03:17 AM||||I'm in Guelph, and could probably help, but you'd have to get here first..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||bHooper||03-30-2001||12:06 PM||||you've gotten some good advice, so far... and you've got a pretty smmmaarrrt fiero fella living near you (jonathon aka slammed fiero). so i would say your doing ok, so far.<P>i have never owned a 4 banger, much less drivin one, but i will back up what's been said so far:<P>1) you can easily and cheaply fix the clunking noise... <B>it is the engine/tranny mounts, guaranteed!</B>  your lookin at about $45 us, to replace all those, except the dog bone.<P>2) the shifting condition of the car could very well be related to the shifter being out of adjustment, but my guess is that its the nature of the beast (my 6 cyl 5spd is a nitemare for anyone but myself to learn how to drive!)... let jonathon give you an honest opinion about this one.<P>either way, i would change the dranny fluid to syncromesh or a nice synthetic 5-30 oil!<P>3)fix the oil leaks, and that should fix the smoking problem.  does the car continue to smoke after it's been drivin for a bit?  or does it goe away?  does it have an odor/taste to it?  the reason i ask this, again 6 cyl experience, these cars produce a fair amount of "visible" exhaust when they are cold, or it is cold outside... mostly it's steam from the exhaust hitting the colder air.<P>4) headliners can be fixed cheaply... i did mine, myself, for $20!  $10 for fabric, and $10 for glue... do a search on that one.<P>5) headlight motors are a real pain in the ass... period!<P>6) replace all the sensors as needed<P>most important, enjoy the car and don't let it drive you crazy!  if you hit a stumbling block... take a break and come back to it.  these are "quirky" cars, but they are fun as &#!!<P><BR>hoop||||reg||14
Z||000019||Bob Englert||03-30-2001||06:10 PM||||It was painful to read you say that you found it easier to rev it up and start in 2nd gear.  Your clutch will have a very short life if you do that regularly.<P>It sound sto me like you paid a reasonable price for the car.  Remember it is 17 years old!<P>I would suggest that you try to prioritize (sp?) the repairs that you think are needed.  Major oil leaks and the clutch would be high on my list.  The valve covers are a constant problem on the 4 cyl.  Get a rubber felpro gasket, NOT the cork gasket.<P>You will find a lot of posts on clutch problems if you do a search here on the forum.  You should check the linkage travel at the clutch arm (slave cylinder to see if it is moving at least 15/16" at full travel.  If not you should flush the fluid and check to clutch pedal.  It should be about 1" higher than the brake pedal at rest.  The 84 came with an aluminum clutch pedal that sometimes would bend and not allow full clutch travel.  <P>Get the fluids and filters changed soon so you can see what you really have.  kind of sets the baseline for the start of your ownership.<P>With a little TLC you can have a nice car that will treat you well.  <P>Good luck....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
